Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

## App.xaml.cs — Hook lifecycle events

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Stopped += (s, e) =>
        {
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", page.ViewModel.DraftText);
        };

        window.Resumed += (s, e) =>
        {
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
                page.ViewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
        };

        window.Destroying += (s, e) =>
        {
            // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
            if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
                Preferences.Set("draft_text", page.ViewModel.DraftText);
        };

        return window;
    }
}
```

## Or use a custom Window subclass (cleaner)

```csharp
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page) { }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", page.ViewModel.DraftText);
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            page.ViewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage page)
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", page.ViewModel.DraftText);
    }
}
```

Return it from `App.xaml.cs`:

```csharp
protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
```

## Key points

| Concern | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Why `Stopped` not `Deactivated`?** | `Deactivated` fires for dialogs/notifications — the app may never actually background |
| **Why also `Destroying`?** | On Android, the back button can skip `Stopped` and go straight to `Destroying` |
| **Why `Preferences`?** | It's fast and synchronous — lifecycle handlers must complete quickly (~1–2s) to avoid ANR/watchdog kills |
| **`Resumed` vs first launch** | `Resumed` only fires after `Stopped`, not on initial launch. Load initial state in your ViewModel constructor |

Use `Preferences` for small strings like draft text. For larger state (images, collections), serialize to a file instead.
